WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.379
Welcome to Calipatria, a desert town near
California's Salton Sea.

00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:07.290
This is the city with the lowest elevation
in the western hemisphere,

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.390
55 metres below sea level.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.240
And here, we've also got a visual demonstration
of just what that means:

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.089
because that white spindly thing is a flagpole,

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:18.410
and the American flag up at the top is flying
above sea level.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:22.010
If it wasn't for all the mountains and hills
between here and the Pacific Ocean,

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:26.320
then we'd be wet,
but that flag would stay dry.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.320
Maybe.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.250
We're dry because no rivers
drain into this basin.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.840
The Salton Sea over there only exists because
some engineers screwed up in 1905

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.500
and diverted a good bit of the Colorado River
in there for a couple of years.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.730
That water is steadily getting saltier and
more polluted as it slowly evaporates,

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:44.760
and California's got to deal with that over
the next couple of decades.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.260
But how do we know we're
55 metres below sea level?

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.470
What is sea level?

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.650
In theory, it is the average height of the
sea around the Earth.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:55.880
But there are some problems with that.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.180
Problem 1: the Earth isn't a sphere.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.120
I mean, it's vaguely a sphere, don't get too
excited, Flat-Earthers.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.590
It's a bit squashed.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:06.520
The force of its own spin makes it bulge by
a few kilometres around the equator.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.110
And when you're defining height above sea
level in metres, that's a lot.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:14.470
So geographers solved that by defining the
Earth as an oblate spheroid.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:19.650
Basically a smooth squashed sphere, just in
fairly precise mathematical terms.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.520
But then there's problem 2: the Earth isn't
one uniform lump of rock.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.920
Bits of the mantle are more dense than others,

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.060
so gravity isn't quite the same everywhere
on Earth's surface.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.850
Which means there's a bit more water sitting
over those bits.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.070
And by a bit more, I mean anything up to 80
metres more.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.600
These aren't small amounts,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.540
that could be the difference between this
city being above and below sea level.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.070
And to complicate things even more,

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:47.010
mountains are big enough that their gravity
attracts the ocean, just a little.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.150
So during the 20th century,

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.909
as scientists and Cold War governments started
to need a global reference system

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:55.690
for spacecraft, and missiles, which were basically
the same thing back then,

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.410
they spent a long time measuring Earth's gravity.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.810
The result is the Earth Gravitational Model,
megabytes in size,

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.620
which asks, in purely mathematical terms:

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.780
if this land wasn't here,
but its gravity was,

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.500
and if there was exactly enough water
to fill in the gaps...

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.520
where would all that water sit?

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:17.390
And how far away would it be from that theoretical
smooth squashed sphere?

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.800
The model was finished in 1996.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.610
It is available to this day on a web site
that seems unchanged since 1996,

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.360
and it's what your phone and GPS use.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.570
There are more recent, more precise models,
but they are hundreds of megabytes in size,

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:32.000
and everyone's agreed that
now we've got a standard,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.810
let's not change it and confuse everyone.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.069
Also, we'd also have to update
for sea level rise,

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:39.910
whether it's best-case centimetres
or worst-case metres.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.959
So saying we're "55 metres below sea level"
is mostly just symbolic.

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:45.840
But so are many things in the world,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.300
and I'd like to think
we still put some trust in them.

